    {
      "id": "claim_f5d2fb00234e4cbb84a20d894ead6e72",
      "statement": "A seam is a swappable capability with three roles: a Service Definition declaring the interface, a Service Provider implementing it, and a Consumer using it (commonly a model-facing tool); a package may combine roles, but one role alone is not a seam, and adding a capability means designing all three.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/architecture.md#L111-L111",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:838e6689abf9923259bf3b34df58a58e410767bc6c67eeebdd7dffc4a3c83260:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_87066fc07d0f4a0bbed036d1a0ab0634",
      "statement": "Seams are why one provider swap changes the whole product: filesystem and subprocess providers share one execution world, so pointing them at a remote sandbox moves Bash, PTY, and LSP with them with no provider forks, and subagent providers vary just as widely behind one interface.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/architecture.md#L113-L113",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:f88fc0a27455e50b3b36e03d752251cc7e864ca9303499050be125e1190d294f:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_569a22a05d2b41d0a3376baccd623ea4",
      "statement": "A service can be a core spine service, a swappable capability seam, or a bundle/composition point; the capability graph shows the package that owns the service declaration, its known implementation packages, and packages that consume the service directly.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/capability-seams.md#L6-L6",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:22be85a38a31d044770f30f648e40644e2156bcb3f4339e96d93698e78c34b5f:"
        }
      ]
    },
